# ProxCloud PVE template workflows: placeholder-only configuration reference.
# NEVER put real cluster details or credentials in this committed example.
# All keys below belong in GitHub Actions REPOSITORY SECRETS, not Variables.
# Settings > Secrets and variables > Actions > Secrets > New repository secret.
# The workflow files read secrets.*; an Actions variable with the same name is not used.
#
# To configure with gh CLI, copy this outside the checkout to a private, protected
# local file, replace required placeholders, and remove unused optional entries:
#   gh secret set --repo mutex-solutions/proxcloud --env-file <private-env-file>
# Do not run that command against this example or commit the populated copy.
# Do not paste secret values into chat, command arguments, logs, or workflow YAML.
# Secrets provide best-effort exact-value log masking, NOT complete confidentiality:
# transformed values/substrings may still appear, and artifact contents are NOT masked.
# The OS workflow strips node/VMID/prune fields from its public manifest artifact.
# Review third-party build output and keep private topology out of public artifacts.
#
# SHARED: required by both vm-templates and k8s-node-templates.
# HTTPS origin + port only; do NOT append /api2/json (both build scripts add it).
PVE_URL="<pve-https-origin-with-port>"
# Dedicated least-privilege image-builder API token; see docs/proxmox-permissions.md.
PVE_TOKEN_ID="<image-builder-user@realm!token-name>"
PVE_TOKEN_SECRET="<image-builder-token-secret>"
# Space-separated node names, NOT comma-separated. One node is sufficient on
# shared VM-disk storage; local VM-disk storage needs a template on every target node.
PVE_NODES="<node-name-1> <node-name-2>"
# PVE storage ID for template VM disks/cloud-init. Must be usable on the target
# nodes; the Kubernetes workflow also downloads its installer ISO here, so that
# workflow needs this storage to allow iso content as well as VM images.
PVE_TEMPLATE_STORAGE="<template-storage-id>"
# Bridge for build VMs, with Internet access and a route back to the runner.
PVE_BRIDGE="<build-bridge-name>"
#
# OS ONLY: required by vm-templates; not used by k8s-node-templates.
# PVE storage ID explicitly allowing import content for downloaded cloud images.
PVE_IMPORT_STORAGE="<import-capable-storage-id>"
# A dedicated unused VM address in CIDR notation, NOT the runner's own IP.
# Reserve/exclude it from DHCP and other allocation; builds reuse it sequentially.
BUILD_IP="<dedicated-build-vm-address>/<network-prefix-length>"
BUILD_GATEWAY="<build-network-gateway-address>"
# Optional OS DNS server. Omit this secret or leave it empty to fall back to
# BUILD_GATEWAY; that gateway must then provide working DNS for the build VM.
# BUILD_NAMESERVER="<build-network-dns-address>"
#
# KUBERNETES ONLY: version is a manual workflow input, not a cluster secret.
# k8s-node-templates requires input version=<kubernetes-version-without-v-prefix>.
# The Ubuntu installer requires DHCP on PVE_BRIDGE. Prefer existing trusted DHCP.
# OPTIONAL: only when that bridge has NO DHCP, set BUILD_DHCP_RANGE as a comma-
# separated start,end pair and set BUILD_GATEWAY above (also required in this mode).
# WARNING: this starts dnsmasq on the runner's DEFAULT-ROUTE interface. The runner
# must be on the SAME L2 network/VLAN as the build bridge; routed API/SSH access
# alone is insufficient. Verify the interface and use an isolated, approved range.
# NEVER enable this alongside a competing DHCP server or overlapping address pool.
# This mode also requires sudo/systemd-run/dnsmasq privileges; runners without sudo
# must use existing DHCP and leave BUILD_DHCP_RANGE unset.
# If both runners are eligible, BOTH must satisfy these network prerequisites.
# BUILD_DHCP_RANGE="<dhcp-range-start-address>,<dhcp-range-end-address>"
#
# EXECUTION SAFETY: these jobs create/clone/start/delete/prune real PVE VMs.
# Use only dedicated self-hosted runners labelled proxcloud-pve on trusted code.
# Never add pull_request, pull_request_target, or another fork/PR-reachable trigger
# to these privileged workflows. Do not route ordinary public-PR CI onto them.
# Labels are routing hints, not a security boundary; restrict runner access and
# protect workflow changes/main. Keep the shared vm-templates concurrency group:
# it serializes OS/Kubernetes builds even when multiple runners are online.
